Revision 12 — 2026-05-16
=========================================================

Summary
---------------------------------------------------------
Fixed 5 bugs from code review: backspace/delete render-sync,
unused HLDB_ENTRIES macro, display loop writing raw tabs to
terminal, and cursor position using wrong col_off space.
---------------------------------------------------------
Detail
---------------------------------------------------------

[1] src/editor.c — editor_delChar: update render buffer on backspace
    Before : editor_update_syntax(ec, row);
    After  : editor_update_row(ec, row);
    Why    : Simple backspace deletes a character but only
             re-ran syntax highlighting, skipping render buffer
             regeneration. When tab_stop differs from the terminal's
             default, the display would show stale tab-expanded
             content. editor_update_row calls editor_update_syntax
             internally, so highlighting is still correct.

[2] src/editor.c — editor_deleteCharAtCursor: update render buffer on delete
    Before : editor_update_syntax(ec, row);
    After  : editor_update_row(ec, row);
    Why    : Same root cause as [1]. Simple delete key re-ran
             syntax only, leaving the render buffer stale.

[3] src/syntax.c — Remove unused HLDB_ENTRIES macro
    Before : #define HLDB_ENTRIES (...)
             const int g_high_light_data_base_entries = (...);
    After  : const int g_high_light_data_base_entries = (...);
    Why    : The #define was never used anywhere in the codebase;
             only the const int variant was used by
             editor_select_syntax_highlight. Removed the dead macro.

[4] src/render.c — Display loop: expand tabs to spaces during render
    The old loop indexed chars directly with col_off + j and wrote
    raw characters (including \t) to the terminal. The terminal
    would expand \t at its own tab stop (usually 8), but the
    editor uses ec->tab_stop (e.g. 4). Causing visual misalignment
    on any line containing a tab when tab_stop != 8.
    Fixed by a two-phase loop:
      Phase 1: skip past col_off using render-space tracking (rx)
      Phase 2: render visible chars with inline tab expansion:
        - \t → emit spaces up to the next tab_stop boundary
        - other chars → write one byte, advance rx
    Syntax highlighting uses chars-space indexing (j), so
    row->high_light[j] remains correct throughout.

[5] src/render.c — Cursor positioning: use render-space col_off_rx
    Before : rx - ec->col_off
    After  : rx - col_off_rx  (where col_off_rx =
              editor_row_cx_to_rx(ec, row, ec->col_off))
    Why    : rx is in render space (after tab expansion), but
             ec->col_off is in chars space (before expansion).
             When tabs existed before the horizontal scroll offset,
             rx - col_off could be negative or wrong, placing the
             cursor at an incorrect column.

Affected Files
---------------------------------------------------------
  src/editor.c          —  2 lines changed
  src/syntax.c          —  2 lines removed
  src/render.c          — ~80 lines changed (display loop rewrite)
                         + 3 lines changed (cursor positioning)

